  <title>
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">Absolute Return for Kids</narrative>
  </title>
  <description>
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">Diarrhoea has been a challenge to child survival in poor countries like Zambia for decades. Although it can be prevented and treated, diarrhoea is responsible for 15,000 deaths of Zambian children each year. It is getting more attention thanks to a new vaccine for rotavirus that can prevent up to one-third of all diarrhoeal deaths, and which is becoming available in some countries. This project will reduce diarrhoeal illness and deaths of children using an integrated approach. The project will take place in the four districts of Lusaka Province, covering about one-sixth of Zambia&apos;s total population. It will provide rotavirus vaccines for children, train health workers to better manage children with diarrhoea, help make drugs to treat diarrhoea more available in the public and private sectors and help families and communities learn to prevent and treat diarrhoea. The project is linked to a research programme to show the effects of this holistic approach on child deaths. Partners are working with government to create and document a model diarrhoea control programme that can be expanded to other parts of the country. This will result in fewer diarrhoeal illnesses and deaths of children under-5 in Zambia.</narrative>
  </description>
